{:edb/db-before-t 157 :edb/db-after-t 158 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533470 50 #inst "2026-10-11T01:13:08.900Z" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186115383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396883 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397103 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397113 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397174 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397188 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397202 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149919 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149922 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149925 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149928 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397123 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397127 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397130 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397133 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152581 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + x^{2}y^{′}(x) + y(x) = 2x, 0 \\le x \\le π$\n\n$2y(0) + y^{′}(0) = 0, y(π) + 2y^{′}(π) = 0$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152581 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149932 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149935 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149938 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149942 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397136 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397140 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397143 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397146 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186152582 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + \\sin (x)y^{′}(x) + 3y(x) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 2$\n\n$y^{′}(0) = 2, y^{′}(2) =-1$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186152582 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396798 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396824 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397012 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397031 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397048 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397067 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186157463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397086 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186166942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396978 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397216 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397229 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186166945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397258 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186166946 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397269 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164747 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164752 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164757 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164762 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396958 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396963 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396968 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396973 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-4}} matrix." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186167268 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $x_{p}(t) = Φu(t)$, where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $x_{p}(t)$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $x_{p}(t) = Φu(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{p}^{′}(t) = Φu^{′}(t) + Φ^{′}u(t)$\n> $L3$: $Φu^{′}(t) + Φ^{′}u(t) = AΦu(t) + f(t)$\n> $L4$: $Φ^{′} = AΦ$\n> $L5$: $Φu^{′}(t) = f(t)$\n> $L6$: $u^{′}(t) = Φ^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n> $L7$: $u(t) = \\displaystyle \\intΦ^{-1}f(t)dt$\n> $L8$: $x_{p}(t) = Φ\\displaystyle \\intΦ^{-1}f(t)dt$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by {{field-1}} both sides of the equation.\n\nLine $L3$ is obtained by substituting $L1$ and $L2$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L5$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from the fact that {{field-4}} matrix." 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186167268 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ To get $\\text{L2},$ we are $\\text{differentiating}$ with respect to $t$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φu(t))^{′} \\\\ {x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396850 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396860 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186221419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396873 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186221427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396901 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186221427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396922 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396735 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396741 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396761 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396777 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396942 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396999 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397155 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397242 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228537 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228540 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397149 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397152 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x\\cos (x)$ on $x ∈ [- π, π]$. Select the correct options below.\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nConsequently, the\n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533470 17592186045585 false] [17592186229727 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186229727 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396736 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396735 1069 "q-335183" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396735 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+7,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)$ |  |  |" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396735 1072 #uuid "9ba8d18b-c5fe-5645-a494-a8fce82f879d" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396735 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7