x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{2+{e}^{-x}}_{⏟}}_{f}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by the $\\text{reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2+{e}^{-x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(2).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the  $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2)\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396826 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396827 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396828 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1077 "field-4" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396826 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396825 1080 #uuid "cc543933-c02b-591f-8cf5-11706b38483b" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396826 1081 "\\text{L4}" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396826 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186396826 1084 #uuid "35c14a88-