$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$ {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397155 1072 #uuid "2dbc0eba-24a7-50e0-964f-4c8949e5821f" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397155 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397157 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397158 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397159 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1077 "field-4" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397157 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397156 1080 #uuid "e847dcfa-8d4c-527b-a527-764fa7af066c" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397157 1081 "has the unique solution $y≡0$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397157 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397157 1084 #uuid "b159e62b-c689-5e20-8226-00fb7f02cbc8" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397158 1081 "has infinitely many solutions" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397158 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397158 1084 #uuid "296d3c7d-583d-5979-b5c1-8a501ca9c153" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397159 1081 "has no solution" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397159 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397159 1084 #uuid "45559ca1-ecc3-53ab-b9cb-735c0349e5ab" 13194139533470 175