 17592186045585 true] [17592186397215 1084 #uuid "da6fc2f4-1dc3-536a-a5eb-cd6619ddf024" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397217 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397223 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1069 "q-338837" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-14\\lambda {y}^{′}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1072 #uuid "ba070694-f520-5989-9c36-fcafe8653bcc" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397216 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-14\\lambda m+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-7\\lambda )(m-7\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=7\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{7\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{7\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{7\\lambda x}+7B\\lambda x{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2B{e}^{14\\lambda }+B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }+\\frac{14}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }(1+\\frac{14}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{14\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{14}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397218 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397219 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397220 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397221 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397222 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1077 "field-2" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397219 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397217 1080 #uuid "6451528f-6193-5fce-847d-24fbb635dd58" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397218 1081 "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397218 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397218 1084 #uuid "6333f256-57a4-524f-882c-85ce13ed4d52" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] [17592186397219 1081 "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" 13194139533470 17592186045585 true] 